Hi [~accountid:5f896ba356eec000768527be],

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
Could you please ask City Legal for a Word version of the agreement? The PDF they shared is difficult to work with.

*Liability:*

Regarding liability, if this aggregates all legal and privacy claims, it poses some risks, but not critical. Our suggestion is to negotiate that the cap applies only to individual claims or series of related claims. Additionally, we want to discuss exceptions for negligence and other issues. Our goal is to provide detailed feedback directly to the DPA, but you can outline our position preliminarily. Perhaps they can propose updated wording in the meantime.
{panel}

Hi [~accountid:62a7061fbf7afc006f3a8000] , I reviewed the agreement from a Privacy POV and also left some comments. I also echo [~accountid:712020:02e8ad5d-a35d-44b9-b445-6111cdb230dc] 's comment: please review all the terms proposed by Aivis to confirm that they align with your understanding and expectations, and that you agree with the conditions set out. 😌 

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
One thing is worth bringing up concerning the agreement:
{panel}

* Aivis’ lawyer has removed the clause requiring the production team to ensure the anonymisation of passengers who do not wish to be filmed, along with the language placing full liability on their side in case of failure. They noted that this clause is “not compatible with the service.”
 ➡️ :red_cross:  We cannot accept the removal of this clause. My suspicion is that the original wording may have been misunderstood to mean that *all* passengers would need to be blurred or have their voices altered, which would indeed be incompatible with the concept of the show. To clarify, I have proposed a revised version of the clause that makes clear anonymisation only applies where individuals should not appear in the final content.
What matters here is that this is fundamentally a liability clause. We are seeking a clear contractual safeguard: if someone who should not have been identifiable ends up appearing in the published footage, the production team bears *full responsibility and liability*, not Bolt. The clause is intended to reflect that risk allocation.

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
Concerning the Privacy Notice shared below:
{panel}

Since this project takes place within the Bolt ride-hailing environment, and riders are likely to associate the filming with Bolt, it is important that the privacy notice presented by Aivis is clear and easy to understand. Although Aivis acts as the independent controller and is fully responsible for their own notice, we recommend ensuring that the following elements are included to reduce confusion and avoid misdirected complaints:

* Aivis/AC Production is identified as the sole controller responsible for the filming and the use of the content;
* Clear contact details (such as an email address) are provided for riders who may have privacy-related questions or concerns;
* It is explained that riders have the right to object or request not to appear in the final content, ideally before publication;
* There is a clear description of what will happen if someone does not wish to appear — for example, whether their footage will be removed or anonymised.

This information does not need to be overly detailed or legalistic. A short and accessible notice is sufficient, as long as it helps riders understand who is responsible and how they can exercise their rights. Including these elements will help reinforce the separation of roles and reduce the likelihood of complaints being directed to Bolt.

Hi [~accountid:63fdee79feb4b150c57651b4] - Privacy completed the questionnaire to the best of our knowledge, but some questions still require assistance from the Infosec function. We included comments regarding this, along with a few internal clarifications and questions about our positions and previous statements. [Please have a look|https://docs.google.com/document/d/1QZqIWi9STCJ8mWFOcZN7cc3PmIcmP_pmI6VlYIIDy84/edit?tab=t.0].

{panel:bgColor=#eae6ff}
As a general note, we recommend flagging questions to privacy early if you are unsure of the answer and cannot find it in the available internal policies or documentation. In this case, the questionnaire was flagged after the second round of review, where some previous statements appear incorrect and required adjustments from privacy in the follow-up response we worked on.
{panel}

